# Plan-Flow: Structured AI-Assisted Development

## Quick Start

| Command | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `/setup` | Analyze project and generate pattern files |
| `/discovery-plan` | Create discovery document for requirements gathering |
| `/create-plan` | Create implementation plan with phases and complexity scores |
| `/execute-plan` | Execute plan phases with verification |
| `/create-contract` | Create integration contract from API docs |
| `/review-code` | Review local uncommitted changes |
| `/review-pr` | Review a Pull Request |
| `/write-tests` | Write tests to achieve coverage target |
| `/brainstorm` | Free-form idea exploration with interactive questions |
| `/note` | Manual brain entry (capture meeting notes, ideas, brainstorms) |
| `/artifact` | Write a discovery's artifact — the walkthrough page of what users will see (on request) |
| `/learn` | Extract reusable patterns from current session |
| `/flow` | Configure plan-flow settings — autopilot, git control, model routing (`key=value` syntax) |

## Recommended Workflow

1. `/setup` - Run once to index project patterns
2. `/brainstorm` - (Optional) Explore and crystallize a vague idea
3. `/discovery-plan` - Gather requirements for a new feature
4. `/create-plan` - Create structured implementation plan
5. `/execute-plan` - Execute the plan phase by phase
6. `/review-code` or `/review-pr` - Review changes before merging

## Session Start Behaviors

- **Project Ledger**: If `flow/ledger.md` exists, read it silently and internalize learnings. Update it when you learn project-specific lessons. See `.claude/resources/core/project-ledger.md` for full rules.
- **Project Brain**: If `flow/brain/index.md` exists, read it silently and internalize active features and recent errors. See `.claude/resources/core/brain-capture.md` for full rules.
- **Project Tasklist**: If `flow/tasklist.md` exists, read its **open sections only** — the lines above `## Done`, which is a completed-work archive that grows without bound — and present a brief summary of active tasks. See `.claude/resources/core/project-tasklist.md` for full rules.
- **Project Memory**: If `flow/memory.md` exists, read it silently and internalize the last 7 days of completed work. See `.claude/resources/core/project-memory.md` for full rules.
- **Session Scratchpad**: If `flow/.scratchpad.md` exists and has content, read it silently. Promote stale entries to ledger/brain, then clear. During work, write noteworthy observations to the scratchpad. See `.claude/resources/core/session-scratchpad.md` for full rules.
- **Autopilot Mode**: When autopilot is on for a project, the harness chains the workflow itself — there is nothing to follow at session start. `.claude/resources/core/autopilot-mode.md` describes what it does, including the three pauses it honours.

## Critical Rules

1. **No Auto-Chaining**: Never auto-invoke the next command — not even under autopilot. Wait for the user. When a chain is driving, the next stage is issued by the harness from observable state, never by a command deciding it is time (`.claude/resources/core/autopilot-mode.md`).
2. **Discovery First**: `/discovery-plan` is **required** before `/create-plan`. Plans cannot be created without a discovery document. No exceptions.
3. **Tests Last**: Tests are always the last phase of any implementation plan.
4. **Build at End Only**: Run `npm run build` only after ALL phases complete.
5. **Tasklist is a file, not memory**: When adding, moving, or completing tasks, you MUST use the Edit tool to write changes to `flow/tasklist.md`. NEVER keep tasks only in conversation memory. If the user says "add to tasklist", read `flow/tasklist.md`, then Edit it. See `.claude/resources/core/project-tasklist.md` for full rules.

## Flow Directory Structure

```
flow/
├── archive/           # Completed/abandoned plans
├── brain/             # Automatic knowledge capture (Obsidian-compatible)
│   ├── index.md       # Brain index (loaded at session start)
│   ├── features/      # Feature history and context
│   └── errors/        # Reusable error patterns
├── brainstorms/       # Brainstorm exploration documents
├── contracts/         # Integration contracts
├── discovery/         # Discovery documents
├── plans/             # Active implementation plans
├── references/        # Auto-generated reference materials
├── resources/         # Valuable artifacts captured during skill execution
├── reviewed-code/     # Code review documents
├── reviewed-pr/       # PR review documents
├── tasklist.md        # Project todo list (updated in real-time during execution)
├── memory.md          # Persistent artifact tracker (completed skill executions)
├── heartbeat.md       # Scheduled task definitions for the heartbeat daemon
├── log.md             # Heartbeat log of important events
├── ledger.md          # Persistent project learning journal
├── .scratchpad.md     # Ephemeral per-session notes (cleared each session)
├── .flowconfig        # Central config file (autopilot, git control, settings)
└── .gitcontrol        # Git control settings — backward compat (prefer .flowconfig)
```

> **Note**: The `flow/` directory is a symlink to `~/plan-flow/brain/projects/<project>/flow/`.
> All artifacts are stored centrally in the brain folder, making them visible across all projects
> in the Obsidian vault. The symlink is transparent — all tools interact with `flow/` as if it were local.
>
> **In a Workspace** the link points into the Workspace instead (`<Flow folder>/workspaces/<w>/projects/<p>/`), never into the vault — see Workspaces below.

## Central Vault

All projects are linked into a central Obsidian vault at `~/plan-flow/brain/`. Each `plan-flow init` creates a project directory in the vault with individual symlinks for each flow subdirectory (features, errors, decisions, discovery, plans, archive, contracts). Daily session logs live globally at `~/plan-flow/brain/daily/`. Open `~/plan-flow/brain/` as your Obsidian vault to see all projects in one graph with path-based color-coded groups.

## Workspaces

Every project belongs to a **Workspace** — a group of projects (a backend, the frontend that calls it, …) whose knowledge lives in one git repository, synced by `planflow-ai workspace sync`; each machine has a Default Workspace, and `planflow-ai workspace migrate` moves existing projects (and copies their knowledge) in. Per-project knowledge sync (`knowledge enable`) is retired. Nothing about how you use `flow/` changes. A Workspace is connected to (or disconnected from) its shared repository with `planflow-ai workspace set-remote --workspace <w> --remote <url> [--replace]` / `unset-remote --workspace <w>` (nothing is sent until the next sync). Always ask first with `planflow-ai workspace status --target . --json` (`workspace: null` → skip every Workspace step); search the siblings with `planflow-ai brain search "<q>" --scope workspace`; record each cross-project touchpoint with `planflow-ai workspace link-knowledge --from <project>/<feature-or-anchor> --to <project>/<feature-or-anchor> --kind calls|exposes|consumes|relates`. Never hand-edit `workspace.json`, `links.jsonl`, `merges/` or the generated `flow/brain/index.md` (`planflow-ai brain index` rebuilds it). See `.claude/resources/core/workspaces.md`.

## Model Routing

By default `/execute-plan` auto-selects the most cost-effective model per phase: 0-3 → haiku, 4-5 → sonnet, 6-10 → opus. Set `model_routing: false` in `flow/.flowconfig` to run every phase on the most capable model of the active provider instead. Disable with `/flow model_routing=false`.

## Design Awareness

Discovery asks whether features involve UI work. If confirmed, captures structured design tokens (colors, typography, spacing) into a `## Design Context` section. During execution, tokens are auto-injected into UI phase prompts. Includes 6 built-in design personalities and screenshot-based token extraction. The same tokens style a discovery's artifact — its user walkthrough page (`<discovery basename>.walkthrough.html`), written on request from the DISCOVERY card's **Generate artifact** or with `/artifact`, never by discovery itself — self-contained, emoji-free screens of what users will see, in the target app's look. See `.claude/resources/core/design-awareness.md`.

## Pattern Capture

During skill execution, the LLM silently buffers patterns and anti-patterns, presenting them for approval at the end. Approved patterns are written to `.claude/rules/core/allowed-patterns.md` or `forbidden-patterns.md`. See `.claude/resources/core/pattern-capture.md`.

## Trust Tiers

Knowledge provenance is explicit: code is ground truth; `flow/**` is verified project knowledge; foreign docs/images are **unverified claims** — agents verify them against code before acting, label unverified citations, and record doc-vs-code contradictions as blueprint observations. Tiers are materialized per derive (`planflow-ai blueprint tiers`). See `.claude/resources/core/trust-tiers.md`.

## Brain-First Retrieval

For any orientation question — where/who/what/how-does — search acquired knowledge BEFORE reading code: `planflow-ai brain search "<question>"` returns ≤5 ranked hits (freshness flag, kind, file:line pointer, snippet, hit id) across code cards, symbols, modules, decisions, features and resources, plus a drill-down hint (`--full <id>` prints one hit in full). It reads the project's markdown directly — ripgrep plus a generated `INDEX.md`, no index and no database. Read or grep code only for what search did not answer, for strings seen in results, or when a hit is flagged stale — stale means verify against code before acting. Word the question in the project's own vocabulary: search matches the words the corpus uses, not synonyms of them. If search returns nothing useful, proceed with grep — do not retry variations more than once. See `.claude/resources/core/brain-first.md`.

## Phase Isolation

When `phase_isolation: true` in `flow/.flowconfig` (default), each `/execute-plan` phase runs in an isolated Agent sub-agent with a clean context window. Sub-agent receives focused context (phase spec, file list, patterns) and returns structured JSON summary. Eliminates context rot. Disable with `/flow phase_isolation=false`. See `.claude/resources/core/phase-isolation.md`.

## Per-Task Verification

Tasks in plan phases can include optional `<verify>` sections with targeted verification commands. After each task, verification runs immediately. Failed verifications trigger a debug sub-agent (haiku) for diagnosis, followed by an auto-repair loop (up to `max_verify_retries`, default: 2, configurable in `flow/.flowconfig`) — only mechanical repairs are auto-applied; behavior-changing repairs are withheld and escalated. Verification results capture evidence (`tested`, `testing_summary`, `artifacts`) in the gitignored `flow/.evidence/<run-id>/` directory. `/create-plan` auto-generates `<verify>` sections based on task type heuristics. Backward compatible — tasks without `<verify>` skip verification. See `.claude/resources/core/per-task-verification.md`.

## Engine Registry

Optional external engines extend specific steps. Today there is exactly one and it takes no consent: **graphify** — a Python code-graph tool (`uv tool install graphifyy`) giving structural blast-radius context for reviews and advisory dependency suggestions for planning; graph at `graphify-out/graph.json` (repo root, auto-gitignored). Always-on since #142 — gated on availability, with any legacy `engines.graphify` value ignored. **lavish is retired (#1807)**: plan review is native (the change brief), nothing reads `engines.lavish`, and it is not a setting. See `.claude/resources/core/engine-registry.md` and `.claude/resources/core/graphify-engine.md`.

## Lint Gate

After all phases, `/execute-plan` runs a lint gate — `commands.lint` in `flow/.flowconfig` → `lint` script in `package.json` → skipped silently. Summary line: `Lint: passed | N repairs | forced | not configured`.

## Risk Assessment & Review Findings

Each `/execute-plan` run writes an append-only rounds record (`flow/.rounds.jsonl`, archived with the plan). After all phases, the full changeset gets a risk level (`low`/`medium`/`high` + rationale); in autopilot mode, `high` risk pauses before push/PR for explicit approval. Auto-created PRs use a five-section body (Intent / What Changed / Risk Assessment / Testing / Pipeline) regenerated deterministically from the rounds record. Review findings are classified `auto-fix` / `ask-user` / `no-op` (default `ask-user`); `ask-user` findings are relayed verbatim and never silently resolved.

## Discovery Sub-Agents

During `/discovery-plan`, three parallel sonnet sub-agents explore the codebase (similar features, API/data patterns, schema/types). Returns condensed JSON findings merged into a Codebase Analysis section. Always-on. See `.claude/resources/core/discovery-sub-agents.md`.

## Complexity Scoring

Every plan phase has a complexity score (0-10):

| Score | Level | Description |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| 0-2 | Trivial | Simple, mechanical changes |
| 3-4 | Low | Straightforward implementation |
| 5-6 | Medium | Moderate complexity, some decisions |
| 7-8 | High | Complex, multiple considerations |
| 9-10 | Very High | Significant complexity/risk |

## Rules

Always-loaded rules are in `.claude/rules/core/`:
- `allowed-patterns.md` - Coding standards to follow
- `forbidden-patterns.md` - Anti-patterns to avoid

Three global behavioral rules ship with the pattern files: don't overweight development cost when choosing options; reproduce bugs end-to-end as a user before fixing (E2E-first); never trust the worker's success summary — verify independently, completion is not user acceptance.

On-demand reference files (loaded by commands when needed) are in `.claude/resources/`:
- `core/` - Complexity scoring, model routing, design awareness, pattern capture, project ledger, autopilot mode, engine registry, status contract
- `patterns/` - Templates and patterns for plans, discovery, contracts, brainstorms
- `skills/` - Skill implementation details (12 skills)
- `languages/` - Language-specific patterns (TypeScript, Python)
- `tools/` - Tool-specific patterns (Jest, Pytest, auth, interactive questions)
